.TH TYPE_SHIT 3 "November 2026" "type_shit" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
type_shit - compile-time C++ type inspection and transformation library
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include "type_shit.h"
.PP
.B namespace ts {
.PP
.BI "    is_same<" T ", " U ">"
.br
.BI "    is_const<" T ">"
.br
.BI "    is_volatile<" T ">"
.br
.BI "    is_void<" T ">"
.br
.BI "    is_integral<" T ">"
.br
.BI "    is_floating_pnt<" T ">"
.br
.BI "    is_arithmetic<" T ">"
.br
.BI "    is_ptr<" T ">"
.br
.BI "    is_ref<" T ">"
.br
.BI "    is_array<" T ">"
.br
.BI "    is_func<" T ">"
.br
.BI "    is_conv<" T ", " U ">"
.PP
.BI "    declval<" T ">()"
.PP
.BI "    rm_ref<" T ">::type"
.br
.BI "    rm_ref_t<" T ">"
.br
.BI "    rm_const<" T ">::type"
.br
.BI "    rm_const_t<" T ">"
.br
.BI "    rm_volatile<" T ">::type"
.br
.BI "    rm_volatile_t<" T ">"
.br
.BI "    decay<" T ">::type"
.br
.BI "    decay_t<" T ">"
.PP
.B }
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B type_shit
is a custom C++ type traits library providing compile-time type inspection, comparison, conversion checking, and type transformation.
.PP
The library operates within the
.B ts
namespace and uses C++ templates, partial specialization, constexpr conversion operators, decltype, and compile-time expression evaluation.
.PP
The library is designed to provide type-related functionality without depending on the standard C++ header
.BR <type_traits> .
.SH TYPE INSPECTION
.SS is_same
.nf
template<typename T, typename U>
struct is_same;
.fi
.PP
Determines whether two types are exactly identical.
Returns true when both types match, including their cv qualifiers.
.SS is_const
.nf
template<typename T>
struct is_const;
.fi
.PP
Determines whether a type has the const qualifier.
.SS is_volatile
.nf
template<typename T>
struct is_volatile;
.fi
.PP
Determines whether a type has the volatile qualifier.
.SS is_void
.nf
template<typename T>
struct is_void;
.fi
.PP
Determines whether a type matches the void specializations.
.IP (bu 2
void
.IP (bu 2
const void
.IP (bu 2
volatile void
.IP (bu 2
const volatile void
.PP
.B NOTE:
The current cv-qualified specializations match other qualified types as well. For example,
.B is_void<const int>
currently evaluates to true.
.SS is_integral
.nf
template<typename T>
struct is_integral;
.fi
.PP
Determines whether a type matches one of the integral types currently implemented:
.IP (bu 2
char
.IP (bu 2
short
.IP (bu 2
int
.IP (bu 2
long
.IP (bu 2
long long
.IP (bu 2
unsigned int
.IP (bu 2
bool
.PP
Other integral types are not yet covered.
.SS is_floating_pnt
.nf
template<typename T>
struct is_floating_pnt;
.fi
.PP
Determines whether a type is one of the following floating-point types:
.IP (bu 2
float
.IP (bu 2
double
.IP (bu 2
long double
.SS is_arithmetic
.nf
template<typename T>
struct is_arithmetic;
.fi
.PP
Determines whether a type is recognized as either integral or floating-point.
Uses
.B is_integral
and
.B is_floating_pnt
internally.
.SS is_ptr
.nf
template<typename T>
struct is_ptr;
.fi
.PP
Determines whether a type is a pointer.
Supports pointer-to-pointer types.
.SS is_ref
.nf
template<typename T>
struct is_ref;
.fi
.PP
Determines whether a type is an lvalue reference or rvalue reference.
.SS is_array
.nf
template<typename T>
struct is_array;
.fi
.PP
Determines whether a type is an array.
Supports bounded and unbounded arrays.
.SS is_func
.nf
template<typename T>
struct is_func;
.fi
.PP
Determines whether a type is a function type.
Function pointers are not classified as function types.
.SS is_conv
.nf
template<typename T, typename U>
struct is_conv;
.fi
.PP
Checks whether an expression of type
.I T
can be implicitly converted to type
.IR U .
.PP
The implementation uses
.B declval
and
.B decltype
alongside overload resolution and SFINAE.
.PP
The current implementation uses a C++20 lambda expression and does not cover every edge case handled by
.BR std::is_convertible .
.SH TYPE UTILITIES
.SS declval
.nf
template<typename T>
T&& declval();
.fi
.PP
Provides a hypothetical expression of type
.B T&&
for use in unevaluated contexts.
.PP
It allows compile-time expressions to inspect types without constructing objects.
.PP
.B WARNING:
The function is declared but not defined. It must not be called in evaluated runtime code. It is intended for use in unevaluated contexts such as
.BR decltype .
.SS rm_ref
.nf
template<typename T>
struct rm_ref;

template<typename T>
using rm_ref_t = typename rm_ref<T>::type;
.fi
.PP
Removes an lvalue or rvalue reference from a type.
.SS rm_const
.nf
template<typename T>
struct rm_const;

template<typename T>
using rm_const_t = typename rm_const<T>::type;
.fi
.PP
Removes the const qualifier from a type.
.SS rm_volatile
.nf
template<typename T>
struct rm_volatile;

template<typename T>
using rm_volatile_t = typename rm_volatile<T>::type;
.fi
.PP
Removes the volatile qualifier from a type.
.SS decay
.nf
template<typename T>
struct decay;

template<typename T>
using decay_t = typename decay<T>::type;
.fi
.PP
Transforms a type by removing references, converting supported array types into pointers, converting supported function types into function pointers, and removing top-level cv qualifiers from other types.
.PP
The current implementation handles bounded arrays, unbounded arrays, and ordinary function types. Additional function-type variations, including noexcept function types, are not yet supported.
.SH RETURN AND VALUE CONVENTIONS
Type inspection traits provide an implicit
.B constexpr
conversion operator to
.BR bool .
.PP
This allows trait objects to be used directly in compile-time conditions.
.PP
Example:
.nf
static_assert(ts::is_same<int, int>{});
static_assert(ts::is_ptr<int*>{});
static_assert(!ts::is_array<int>{});
.fi
.PP
Type transformation traits expose their resulting type through the nested member
.BR type .
.PP
The corresponding alias templates provide direct access without explicitly writing
.BR ::type .
.SH IMPLEMENTATION NOTES
The library currently implements its own type inspection and transformation mechanisms using templates and compile-time expressions.
.PP
The implementation is a work in progress. Trait coverage and compatibility with the C++ standard library are not guaranteed.
.SH FILES
.I type_shit.h
.RS
Main header containing all current type traits and utilities.
.RE
.SH DEPENDENCIES
No C++ standard library headers are required by the current implementation.
